all this week we've focused on the  rising fire risk in the west the extreme  drought and the threat to farming and  wildlife  this morning five wildfires are burning  in utah and the governor has declared a  drought emergency there  the great salt lake is expected to reach  its lowest point in modern history  this year jonathan bigliotti shows how  the lack of water affects lives  and livelihoods in utah where many  farmers are struggling  just to keep their crops alive i don't  know  how long we can survive because i don't  know how bad it's going to get  on his property north of salt lake city  sixth generation farmer  ron gibson is covering his crops with  plastic  to conserve water his water allocation  was cut  by 70 percent if we don't get these  crops to market  we can't survive he also says the cost  of feeding his dairy cows  has doubled in a year when agriculture  is gone then our ability to produce food  is gone that's what scares me the most  about this whole situation  the entire state of utah is experiencing  drought  more than 90 percent in an extreme  category  we would be standing under water had  it been a normal water year we met zach  frankel  executive director of the utah rivers  council at the great salt lake  90 percent of our precipitation comes in  the winter and snow  as our winter air temperatures continue  to rise  we're getting less and less snow in our  mountains utah governor spencer cox has  declared  a state of emergency and called on  people in utah  to reduce their water use and pray for  rain  we need some divine intervention so when  the governor says  pray for rain what do you say to that i  say  the lord helps those who help themselves  and the question is  are state leaders willing to accept that  this is not a short-term drought  but that this is long-term climate  change  utah is the second driest state in the  country  but has some of the highest public water  consumption  per capita back on the farm gibson says  a continued water crisis means an  uncertain future  your family's been doing this for 152  years that's many generations do you  worry that this  is the last generation of farming in  your family you know i can't sleep at  night  and most nights because that's exactly  the concern  we're told this marina would normally be  floating on about 10  feet of water but like much of the state  it has dried up  utah feeds the colorado river and less  water in this state  means trouble downstream impacting  nearly 40 million people  in seven states for cbs this morning  jonathan vigliotti antelope island  in utah's great salt lake i don't know  how many more alarm bells you need you  know no  no i feel for him saying i can't sleep  at night next time we complain about  heavy rain think about the people that  would  really kill to have a big trench and  next time you open your fridge and you  see  fruit and you see vegetables think about  the farmers who are bringing that to us  i can't imagine being a farmer and  imagining like 150 years of  family farming may be coming to an end  here
 

